1
00:00:09,884 --> 00:00:12,970
- Sorry he woke you.
- Oh, it wasn't him.

2
00:00:13,930 --> 00:00:15,306
Was I snoring again?

3
00:00:15,598 --> 00:00:17,350
I thought it was a low-flying jet.

4
00:00:22,271 --> 00:00:24,232
- Thanks for playing with him.
- I don't mind.

5
00:00:25,274 --> 00:00:27,985
I had to get up early anyway to drive
home and get changed for work.

6
00:00:28,110 --> 00:00:29,737
Yeah. He's a good kid.

7
00:00:29,862 --> 00:00:32,823
You know, I'd like to stay at your place
but when I have Benjamin, it's...

8
00:00:33,908 --> 00:00:35,576
I know. It's fine.

9
00:00:37,537 --> 00:00:39,622
You know, you should be
careful, lady.

10
00:00:39,872 --> 00:00:41,916
I could still make
an honest woman of you yet.

11
00:00:43,584 --> 00:00:44,961
You actually could.

12
00:00:46,462 --> 00:00:48,256
Did you hear about
last month's policy change?

13
00:00:48,673 --> 00:00:51,259
Oh, yeah. No more sanctions
on office relationships.

14
00:00:51,384 --> 00:00:53,302
Yeah. Read the e-mail.

15
00:00:54,262 --> 00:00:56,681
Right. As long
as there's no rank issues.

16
00:00:57,515 --> 00:01:00,685
Right. So, I, uh...
I couldn't date Lisbon.

17
00:01:01,143 --> 00:01:06,190
- Right. But do you wanna date Lisbon?
- No. Ooh. God, no. I just...

18
00:01:06,941 --> 00:01:08,317
No.

19
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:12,905
They're even letting agents
get married.

20
00:01:13,030 --> 00:01:14,407
"That's awesome."

21
00:01:16,409 --> 00:01:18,286
- You wanna shower first?
- Oh...

22
00:01:18,578 --> 00:01:20,037
- You go.
- All right.

23
00:01:30,089 --> 00:01:32,425
Lisbon. Good.
We're going to Napa. You drive.

24
00:01:33,759 --> 00:01:36,012
- Homicide bulletins?
- Hot off the press.

25
00:01:36,137 --> 00:01:38,347
A John Doe in a duck pond awaits.

26
00:01:38,472 --> 00:01:40,641
Well, that sounds like a case
for local authorities.

27
00:01:40,766 --> 00:01:42,143
Bingo.

28
00:01:43,060 --> 00:01:46,731
Napa. Okay, wait a minute.
That's Sheriff McAllister's jurisdiction.

29
00:01:46,856 --> 00:01:48,816
You wanna go and investigate
a Red John suspect.

30
00:01:48,941 --> 00:01:51,694
Investigating is your thing. I'd just like
to get to know him a little better.

31
00:01:51,819 --> 00:01:53,195
Shall we?

32
00:02:17,970 --> 00:02:20,348
Gardening crew found
the body submerged in the pond.

33
00:02:21,307 --> 00:02:24,268
Doesn't look like he was in the
water for more than a few hours.

34
00:02:24,393 --> 00:02:26,228
There's a contusion
on the back of the skull.

35
00:02:26,520 --> 00:02:28,314
I'm guessing someone
caught him from behind.

36
00:02:28,439 --> 00:02:31,233
What was he doing behind a church
in the middle of the night?

37
00:02:31,651 --> 00:02:33,110
Any personal belongings?

38
00:02:33,235 --> 00:02:36,113
No wallet or cell phone.
Just a money roll in his front pocket.

39
00:02:36,238 --> 00:02:37,782
$32 in small bills.

40
00:02:40,701 --> 00:02:42,078
Hey, Jane?

41
00:02:42,745 --> 00:02:44,538
Care to offer your opinion?

42
00:02:44,997 --> 00:02:47,041
These ducks love muffins.

43
00:02:47,667 --> 00:02:50,544
- Very helpful.
- Look at 'em go!

44
00:02:52,213 --> 00:02:55,132
I see the cavalry has arrived.

45
00:02:55,383 --> 00:02:58,928
- Sheriff McAllister.
- Agent Lisbon. What, uh...

46
00:02:59,553 --> 00:03:02,723
What brings your crack team
to our little slice of heaven?

47
00:03:02,848 --> 00:03:05,601
Oh, you know, any excuse
to get up to wine country.

48
00:03:05,935 --> 00:03:09,772
Well, we always welcome the help
but I expect I can handle this one.

49
00:03:09,897 --> 00:03:13,234
Well, to be honest, it was, uh...
it was me that wanted to come out here.

50
00:03:13,401 --> 00:03:15,611
- Is that a fact?
- Well, more of a feeling.

51
00:03:15,736 --> 00:03:17,154
I think there's more out here
than meets the eye.

52
00:03:17,279 --> 00:03:18,906
- How so?
- You don't feel it?

53
00:03:19,031 --> 00:03:21,867
- Mm, no.
- What's your take?

54
00:03:21,992 --> 00:03:24,245
- On what?
- Our friend.

55
00:03:24,453 --> 00:03:28,499
Well, let's have a look.
"One five six."

56
00:03:28,624 --> 00:03:30,292
You suppose he's repping his code?

57
00:03:30,876 --> 00:03:35,214
Area code 1-5-6 is reserved
for telemarketers

58
00:03:35,339 --> 00:03:36,924
so I'm guessing no.

59
00:03:37,049 --> 00:03:40,803
Well... he's kind of
a slack-looking fella.

60
00:03:40,928 --> 00:03:42,847
Gloomy, even for a corpse.

61
00:03:42,972 --> 00:03:46,058
You know, cheap clothes,
no muscle tone.

62
00:03:47,268 --> 00:03:49,854
Uh, sedentary low-income job.

63
00:03:51,021 --> 00:03:53,023
- Sounds right.
- Your turn.

64
00:03:56,193 --> 00:03:58,821
The tread on his shoes
wrap up around the heel

65
00:03:58,946 --> 00:04:02,575
to prevent the heel from slipping
like those of a serious race car driver

66
00:04:02,700 --> 00:04:06,454
or a delivery man that likes
to pretend he races cars.

67
00:04:06,996 --> 00:04:10,458
Couple that with the roll of small bills
found in his pant pocket...

68
00:04:10,916 --> 00:04:12,460
Pizza delivery man.

69
00:04:13,169 --> 00:04:15,421
Could be Mexican.
Or ribs.

70
00:04:15,588 --> 00:04:16,630
Could be.

71
00:04:17,339 --> 00:04:18,466
But, uh...

72
00:04:18,716 --> 00:04:22,178
you don't detect the faint scent
of oregano about him?

73
00:04:25,848 --> 00:04:26,891
That's a leap.

74
00:04:40,577 --> 00:04:41,995
What do you think?

75
00:04:42,579 --> 00:04:44,498
Well, he's not as dumb as he looks.

76
00:04:44,957 --> 00:04:46,333
Well, is he Red John?

77
00:04:46,875 --> 00:04:49,378
- It's too early to say.
- How do we find out?

78
00:04:50,295 --> 00:04:53,257
- I need to be alone with him.
- Well, that would not be wise.

79
00:04:54,758 --> 00:04:56,134
What's cookin' in here?

80
00:04:57,010 --> 00:04:58,512
{\an8}Spot any clues?

81
00:04:59,304 --> 00:05:03,809
{\an8}No. Well, just that hammer up there.
You see it?

82
00:05:04,476 --> 00:05:07,020
Is that blood on the hitting side?

83
00:05:12,860 --> 00:05:15,320
{\an8}Could be.
I'll call forensics.

84
00:05:16,071 --> 00:05:17,990
{\an8}- Fellas?
- "Yeah."

85
00:05:18,407 --> 00:05:19,950
- We need a little help...
- Did you see that?

86
00:05:20,075 --> 00:05:22,244
He did not wanna go
up that ladder.

87
00:05:22,369 --> 00:05:25,539
Uh, didn't the psychiatrist's office say
that Red John was afraid of heights?

88
00:05:25,664 --> 00:05:27,291
That's very astute, Lisbon.

89
00:05:28,458 --> 00:05:29,710
{\an8}Well observed.

90
00:05:32,921 --> 00:05:35,215
{\an8}They'll join us
when they're done outside.

91
00:05:35,382 --> 00:05:36,884
{\an8}Yeah, I can't wait that long.

92
00:05:37,009 --> 00:05:39,428
{\an8}Sheriff, would you mind going up
the ladder for me?

93
00:05:41,013 --> 00:05:44,975
{\an8}You want me to climb all the way up there
in these pointed toe boots?

94
00:05:46,894 --> 00:05:48,729
{\an8}That's a job for sensible shoes.

95
00:05:48,854 --> 00:05:51,607
{\an8}- It would be a big help.
- I got it, boss.

96
00:05:54,192 --> 00:05:56,278
- Thank you, son.
- Hey, boss

97
00:05:56,486 --> 00:05:59,907
{\an8}Cho got a D.M.V. hit
on the victim's prints.

98
00:06:00,032 --> 00:06:02,910
{\an8}His name is Larry Kincaid,
46, clean record.

99
00:06:04,202 --> 00:06:07,372
No. Doesn't look like blood.
I think it's paint.

100
00:06:07,497 --> 00:06:08,790
{\an8}Thanks, Rigsby.

101
00:06:08,957 --> 00:06:12,044
{\an8}Kincaid lived in Vacaville
and worked as a pizza delivery driver.

102
00:06:12,586 --> 00:06:14,504
What was the victim
doing in Napa?

103
00:06:14,630 --> 00:06:17,591
His boss said he was here
for his niece's wedding tonight.

104
00:06:17,841 --> 00:06:20,594
{\an8}Stacey Bonner. The rehearsal dinner
was here last night.

105
00:06:20,719 --> 00:06:23,263
{\an8}All right, you two stay in the area
and look for witnesses.

106
00:06:23,388 --> 00:06:25,098
{\an8}We've got a wedding party
to find.

107
00:06:33,690 --> 00:06:36,485
{\an8}Use the table to help you swirl.
The table's your friend.

108
00:06:37,444 --> 00:06:39,196
{\an8}You look so sexy
when you do that.

109
00:06:39,321 --> 00:06:42,616
{\an8}This draws oxygen into the wine
to bring out the aroma.

110
00:06:44,284 --> 00:06:46,453
{\an8}Who cares what it smells like?
Let's drink.

111
00:06:47,454 --> 00:06:51,500
{\an8}Excuse me. Uh, uh, is this
the Bonner-salas wedding party?

112
00:06:51,625 --> 00:06:53,585
I'm Stacey Bonner.
Can I help you?

113
00:06:53,710 --> 00:06:57,798
{\an8}Yes, I'm agent Teresa Lisbon
with the CBI. This is Patrick Jane.

114
00:06:58,423 --> 00:07:03,178
{\an8}We found a body behind the Bella Vista
wedding chapel this morning.

115
00:07:03,303 --> 00:07:06,682
{\an8}It's a member of your party.
His name is Larry Kincaid.

116
00:07:06,807 --> 00:07:09,434
{\an8}Uncle Larry?
Oh, no.

117
00:07:10,686 --> 00:07:13,063
Well... don't everyone cry at once.

118
00:07:13,939 --> 00:07:16,900
{\an8}My wife's brother was something
of a courtesy invite.

119
00:07:17,275 --> 00:07:21,530
{\an8}He's a nice enough guy, but...
a little off, socially speaking.

120
00:07:21,989 --> 00:07:23,073
{\an8}Okay.

121
00:07:23,657 --> 00:07:28,662
{\an8}Uh, you're the bride's father, I take it.
And, uh, where's your wife?

122
00:07:29,121 --> 00:07:30,706
{\an8}She's resting back at the hotel.

123
00:07:31,164 --> 00:07:33,208
{\an8}She came down with a migraine
last night.

124
00:07:33,333 --> 00:07:36,503
Poor uncle Larry.
Mom's gonna freak out.

125
00:07:36,628 --> 00:07:39,047
{\an8}- It's okay, cariña.
- The wedding is ruined!

126
00:07:39,881 --> 00:07:42,634
- What happened to him?
- That's what we're trying to find out.

127
00:07:42,759 --> 00:07:46,430
- Can I see the body?
- Suzie, that's not very appropriate.

128
00:07:46,722 --> 00:07:48,473
This is life happening.

129
00:07:48,765 --> 00:07:50,475
{\an8}We can't shut our eyes.

130
00:07:51,309 --> 00:07:53,937
{\an8}- So... can I look at it?
- No.

131
00:07:54,563 --> 00:07:57,899
{\an8}Who was the last person
who saw the victim alive?

132
00:07:58,483 --> 00:07:59,735
{\an8}All of us.

133
00:07:59,860 --> 00:08:02,404
{\an8}After the rehearsal dinner
we all hit up the big party tent.

134
00:08:02,529 --> 00:08:04,698
- The wedding Pavillion.
- The wedding Pavillion.

135
00:08:05,532 --> 00:08:07,159
It's the big tent by the chapel.

136
00:08:07,284 --> 00:08:10,787
We ran out of wine, and Larry offered
to make an alcohol run.

137
00:08:11,496 --> 00:08:14,249
I'm Mr. Salas.
Roberto's my son.

138
00:08:14,708 --> 00:08:18,503
- Did uncle Larry ever come back?
- No. But we weren't surprised.

139
00:08:18,628 --> 00:08:21,048
It felt like he was looking
for an excuse to leave the party.

140
00:08:21,173 --> 00:08:24,968
Yeah. It's like he was a ghost
before he was a ghost.

141
00:08:25,093 --> 00:08:28,096
Angel, you're such an idiot.
He was her uncle.

142
00:08:29,097 --> 00:08:31,391
I'm sorry, baby.
He seemed like an okay guy.

143
00:08:31,516 --> 00:08:33,226
I'm not crying
about uncle Larry.

144
00:08:35,395 --> 00:08:36,646
It is the wedding day.

145
00:08:39,649 --> 00:08:42,110
Hey, we're still gonna get married,
right? Right?

146
00:08:43,153 --> 00:08:47,741
- I guess. But it's all so... sordid.
- Yeah.

147
00:08:55,082 --> 00:08:58,085
Lisbon said this was the last room
the victim was seen alive in.

148
00:08:58,502 --> 00:08:59,878
Well, it's gonna be
a beautiful wedding.

149
00:09:00,128 --> 00:09:01,880
Eh, I can think of better ways
to spend good money.

150
00:09:02,005 --> 00:09:04,007
Oh, yeah? Like what?
A new leather sectional?

151
00:09:04,132 --> 00:09:05,175
No.

152
00:09:06,009 --> 00:09:07,469
Okay, how'd you guess?

153
00:09:09,221 --> 00:09:11,389
I used to dream about having
a Cinderella wedding.

154
00:09:11,973 --> 00:09:13,892
You did?
You never said anything.

155
00:09:14,017 --> 00:09:15,894
Ah. It was a long time ago.

156
00:09:16,603 --> 00:09:18,563
I suppose it all seems silly now.

157
00:09:29,491 --> 00:09:30,534
Police.

158
00:09:31,952 --> 00:09:33,161
Who's in here?

159
00:09:37,040 --> 00:09:38,416
Step out slowly.

160
00:09:39,292 --> 00:09:40,335
Don't shoot.

161
00:09:40,919 --> 00:09:42,212
Set it down.

162
00:09:42,754 --> 00:09:45,549
- Hands behind your back.
- It's not what you think.

163
00:09:50,428 --> 00:09:51,471
What's this?

164
00:09:55,934 --> 00:09:59,146
Laxatives in the wedding cake, huh?
That's a cute move.

165
00:09:59,604 --> 00:10:01,356
- What's your name?
- Oscar Salas.

166
00:10:01,481 --> 00:10:02,774
The groom's brother.

167
00:10:03,483 --> 00:10:05,318
Why'd you wanna ruin
your brother's wedding, Oscar?

168
00:10:05,819 --> 00:10:07,946
- Why not?
- You don't like his fiancée?

169
00:10:08,196 --> 00:10:11,032
Twitter freak.
Completely self-obsessed.

170
00:10:11,158 --> 00:10:14,161
They all are. Children who need
constant feedback.

171
00:10:14,911 --> 00:10:19,207
Just wait until they try that cake.
I am dying to read that tweet.

172
00:10:20,750 --> 00:10:22,043
What about uncle Larry?

173
00:10:22,544 --> 00:10:25,088
He seems cool.
At least he doesn't follow the pack.

174
00:10:25,213 --> 00:10:27,799
Did you know he was murdered last night
after the rehearsal dinner?

175
00:10:28,842 --> 00:10:31,636
- No.
- Any idea who might wanna hurt him?

176
00:10:32,971 --> 00:10:36,558
Yeah. Angel.
My brother's best man.

177
00:10:36,683 --> 00:10:38,602
He talked trash about Larry
all the time.

178
00:10:39,060 --> 00:10:40,896
I think he sent him
text messages, too.

179
00:10:42,022 --> 00:10:44,858
- What was your beef with the victim?
- No beef.

180
00:10:45,275 --> 00:10:47,694
- We heard you didn't like him.
- You heard wrong.

181
00:10:47,861 --> 00:10:50,405
I didn't have no opinion of him.
Barely knew the guy.

182
00:10:51,573 --> 00:10:53,617
We also heard you harassed him
with text messages.

183
00:10:53,742 --> 00:10:55,118
That's not true.

184
00:10:56,953 --> 00:10:59,998
- How about you show me those texts?
- I'm not giving you my phone.

185
00:11:00,123 --> 00:11:01,541
I can have a court order here
in 30 minutes.

186
00:11:01,666 --> 00:11:03,793
Only then, I'll be looking at you
as a suspect.

187
00:11:14,221 --> 00:11:15,805
"Hey, Larry, any word yet
from your boss?"

188
00:11:15,931 --> 00:11:18,350
"I could really use the work.
Thanks, buddy."

189
00:11:19,851 --> 00:11:23,146
- He was helping you get a pizza job?
- I need work. So what?

190
00:11:23,647 --> 00:11:25,440
- You get the job?
- Nope.

191
00:11:26,107 --> 00:11:28,610
Did you leave the wedding tent
at any point during the after party?

192
00:11:28,944 --> 00:11:31,196
No, I was there till the end.
Ask anyone.

193
00:11:32,030 --> 00:11:34,699
And Larry was long gone
by the time I left.

194
00:11:37,202 --> 00:11:39,329
Thank you for coming,
Mrs. Bonner.

195
00:11:40,372 --> 00:11:42,791
Did you happen to see
your brother leave the party?

196
00:11:43,250 --> 00:11:45,627
No, I, uh...
I had a headache.

197
00:11:46,127 --> 00:11:48,838
I left the party early and walked back
to the honeymoon suite.

198
00:11:49,172 --> 00:11:52,133
Why were you
in the honeymoon suite?

199
00:11:53,969 --> 00:11:56,221
We're paying for this wedding.

200
00:11:57,097 --> 00:11:59,099
What was the victim doing
at the party?

201
00:12:00,183 --> 00:12:04,187
The usual.
Standing... watching.

202
00:12:06,189 --> 00:12:08,650
To be honest, I felt sorry for the man.

203
00:12:10,402 --> 00:12:12,570
What was your relationship
with the victim?

204
00:12:14,698 --> 00:12:16,408
Larry's my fiancée's uncle

205
00:12:16,533 --> 00:12:18,285
so as far as I'm concerned,
he's my blood.

206
00:12:19,744 --> 00:12:21,705
But weird blood, you know?

207
00:12:21,830 --> 00:12:24,749
Do you know of anybody who may
have wanted to harm the victim?

208
00:12:25,583 --> 00:12:28,211
Nobody really felt strongly enough
about him one way or the other.

209
00:12:28,503 --> 00:12:33,383
He just kinda... floated along
like family flotsam.

210
00:12:35,218 --> 00:12:36,761
That was his strength, you know?

211
00:12:37,554 --> 00:12:39,556
The power of social invisibility.

212
00:12:41,057 --> 00:12:42,559
I think I could've loved him.

213
00:12:43,560 --> 00:12:45,729
Did he talk to anyone
at the party?

214
00:12:46,146 --> 00:12:48,857
Yes. My son's friend Charlie
from college.

215
00:12:49,316 --> 00:12:50,942
Did you know Roberto
went to college?

216
00:12:51,526 --> 00:12:52,902
First in the family.

217
00:12:53,570 --> 00:12:55,071
Well, I was pretty toasted.

218
00:12:55,488 --> 00:12:57,532
But I think Larry was talking
about a new rifle.

219
00:12:57,657 --> 00:12:59,242
Said he bought it
to defend himself.

220
00:12:59,367 --> 00:13:00,952
Rifle? Are you sure?

221
00:13:01,077 --> 00:13:04,122
The victim doesn't have
any record of gun ownership.

222
00:13:04,372 --> 00:13:07,375
I think he was just trying to fit in.
You know, be one of the guys.

223
00:13:07,542 --> 00:13:09,586
Same reason Larry offered to go
on a liquor run for us.

224
00:13:09,961 --> 00:13:12,005
Did you see the victim
leave the party?

225
00:13:14,341 --> 00:13:15,800
"I don't speak English."

226
00:13:18,219 --> 00:13:19,262
"Excuse me."

227
00:13:26,561 --> 00:13:29,397
Somebody in that wedding party
had to have seen something.

228
00:13:29,981 --> 00:13:31,900
Love is in the air, Lisbon.

229
00:13:32,359 --> 00:13:34,486
These two families
are about to be married.

230
00:13:34,611 --> 00:13:36,905
They're not gonna turn on each other
without a push.

231
00:13:37,364 --> 00:13:38,406
What kinda push?

232
00:13:39,366 --> 00:13:42,577
If we're gonna learn who killed uncle Larry
then we need to clear the air.

233
00:13:42,702 --> 00:13:45,246
That means no more playing nice
for the happy couple.

234
00:13:46,164 --> 00:13:47,665
We need some honesty.

235
00:13:47,791 --> 00:13:49,959
- Who's not being honest?
- Well, you, for starters.

236
00:13:50,085 --> 00:13:52,545
- You're the groom's sister, I take it.
- And my bridesmaid.

237
00:13:52,670 --> 00:13:53,755
Yes, that was a lovely gesture

238
00:13:53,880 --> 00:13:56,549
but you put her in a very awkward
position because she resents you.

239
00:13:56,674 --> 00:13:59,260
- No, I don't.
- Yes, you do. Be honest.

240
00:13:59,386 --> 00:14:00,762
Do you resent me, Rose?

241
00:14:01,304 --> 00:14:04,808
You talk down to my brother!
You're always correcting him.

242
00:14:04,933 --> 00:14:07,268
- That's not true. When has she ever...
- Not now, Roberto.

243
00:14:07,852 --> 00:14:11,648
Rose, are you mad at me because
I paid for your bridesmaid dress?

244
00:14:12,941 --> 00:14:15,777
- You didn't pay for my dress.
- You don't work in retail.

245
00:14:15,902 --> 00:14:17,278
Now she's doing it to me.

246
00:14:17,404 --> 00:14:20,448
Stacey was an English major, chica.
She just sounds superior.

247
00:14:20,573 --> 00:14:23,118
Chica? My daughter
is not your housemaid.

248
00:14:23,243 --> 00:14:24,577
Hey. She speaks English.

249
00:14:24,702 --> 00:14:26,913
Can we all show
some respect here, please?

250
00:14:27,205 --> 00:14:29,999
- We're a family now.
- Yes, one big, happy family.

251
00:14:30,125 --> 00:14:32,752
Is that why you hooked up
with the bride's mother at the after party?

252
00:14:33,420 --> 00:14:34,462
Hmm? What was that?

253
00:14:34,587 --> 00:14:36,548
You said that your wife
had a migraine last night

254
00:14:36,673 --> 00:14:39,050
which is the enablers' code
for "drunk".

255
00:14:39,175 --> 00:14:42,220
And today, she can't look
the groom's father in the eye.

256
00:14:42,971 --> 00:14:45,306
That's why she wasn't
at the wine tasting this morning.

257
00:14:45,598 --> 00:14:47,725
You made a move on my mom?

258
00:14:47,851 --> 00:14:49,894
Why are you blaming Papi?
Maybe your mom's the adulterer.

259
00:14:50,019 --> 00:14:51,980
- Adulteress.
- Stop correcting him!

260
00:14:52,105 --> 00:14:54,107
- Yeah! I see it now.
- Okay, shut up!

261
00:14:55,650 --> 00:15:00,280
This is my wedding day,
and I will say and do whatever I want.

262
00:15:01,072 --> 00:15:04,617
And what I want...
is to cancel this wedding.

263
00:15:06,744 --> 00:15:08,580
Life. Happening.

264
00:15:08,705 --> 00:15:11,332
Stacey, you don't mean that.
You guys have been planning this for months.

265
00:15:11,458 --> 00:15:13,001
I flew in from Florida to be here.

266
00:15:14,794 --> 00:15:16,212
You're all on your own.

267
00:15:17,046 --> 00:15:18,298
Nice push.

268
00:15:18,423 --> 00:15:20,508
Ah, something good will come
outta this. You'll see.

269
00:15:20,633 --> 00:15:22,385
- Can we go back to Napa now?
- Please.

270
00:15:27,672 --> 00:15:29,924
Groom's brother said that neither side
of the family approves of the wedding

271
00:15:30,049 --> 00:15:31,717
but they're both pretending
like they do.

272
00:15:31,843 --> 00:15:34,554
- Not anymore.
- I followed up on that rifle tip.

273
00:15:34,679 --> 00:15:38,182
According to bank records, uncle Larry wrote
a personal check for one two weeks ago.

274
00:15:38,349 --> 00:15:40,685
We need contact information
for the firearms dealer.

275
00:15:40,935 --> 00:15:44,355
A private rancher sold it to him.
James Harris. I have an address.

276
00:15:44,814 --> 00:15:47,024
The victim said he brought a rifle
to protect himself.

277
00:15:47,150 --> 00:15:48,901
Let's find out from who.
Rigsby, you're with me.

278
00:15:49,026 --> 00:15:50,862
I'll run background checks
on the invitation list.

279
00:15:58,119 --> 00:16:01,456
I thought you were supposed to solve
a crime not, uh, destroy a wedding.

280
00:16:01,914 --> 00:16:03,708
Well, I'd say the killer did that.

281
00:16:04,750 --> 00:16:07,837
I'm prepared to marvel at your insight.
Who's the killer?

282
00:16:08,129 --> 00:16:09,380
Still working on it.

283
00:16:09,505 --> 00:16:11,215
Really? I'm surprised. I'd have...

284
00:16:11,883 --> 00:16:14,093
I'd have thought you'd have him
in cuffs by now.

285
00:16:14,218 --> 00:16:16,053
Oh, I hate to disappoint you.

286
00:16:17,054 --> 00:16:19,765
Jane. Mrs. Bonner would like
to have a word with you.

287
00:16:23,603 --> 00:16:24,937
You were right.

288
00:16:25,146 --> 00:16:27,857
Before I left the party,
Mr. Salas and I shared a kiss.

289
00:16:28,483 --> 00:16:30,610
A drunken, mistaken kiss.

290
00:16:32,320 --> 00:16:35,281
Uh, where did this, uh, inebriated
smooch take place?

291
00:16:36,449 --> 00:16:40,411
- Behind the chapel.
- Did you see anything here by the pond?

292
00:16:40,870 --> 00:16:43,331
No, but we heard a splash
around midnight.

293
00:16:43,998 --> 00:16:47,043
Did you hear any...
screams or sounds of a struggle?

294
00:16:48,002 --> 00:16:49,795
I should've gone to help him.

295
00:16:49,921 --> 00:16:53,049
It wouldn't have mattered. It seems
he was killed someplace else.

296
00:16:53,174 --> 00:16:54,717
The body was just dumped here.

297
00:16:55,551 --> 00:16:58,221
Hiding the body until
after the wedding in the pond

298
00:16:58,346 --> 00:17:01,766
which is not such a great hiding
place, I would think. A little too public.

299
00:17:02,767 --> 00:17:05,478
Bella Vista.
Bella Vista.

300
00:17:06,646 --> 00:17:10,024
"Beautiful view" in Italian.
Where is this, uh, Bella Vista?

301
00:17:11,526 --> 00:17:12,777
Through the trees.

302
00:17:22,787 --> 00:17:24,288
Wow.

303
00:17:24,413 --> 00:17:26,415
Now this is a place to dump a body.

304
00:17:27,792 --> 00:17:29,252
Take a look. Huh?

305
00:17:29,377 --> 00:17:30,920
Killer hauls the body to the pond.

306
00:17:31,254 --> 00:17:34,215
Just a little bit further
and he could've dumped it right here.

307
00:17:34,340 --> 00:17:36,842
He's right. Nobody's finding
anything down there.

308
00:17:37,843 --> 00:17:39,262
Come over here, fella. Have a look.

309
00:17:39,762 --> 00:17:41,430
Yeah, I'm familiar with the view.

310
00:17:44,267 --> 00:17:46,060
Uh, tell us what we're looking at.

311
00:17:46,644 --> 00:17:49,021
- "Sheriff, what's your location..."
- Excuse me. I'm gonna take this.

312
00:17:49,647 --> 00:17:54,151
- Yeah I'm close, I'll head...
- Strike two. You believe me now?

313
00:18:06,455 --> 00:18:09,041
Hey, Cho, you think
I'm a marrying type?

314
00:18:09,875 --> 00:18:11,377
You proposed to your baby mama.

315
00:18:11,502 --> 00:18:14,130
Well, yeah, but every kid deserves
two parents.

316
00:18:15,047 --> 00:18:17,008
I was talking about Van Pelt.

317
00:18:17,466 --> 00:18:19,218
- You knock her up, too?
- No.

318
00:18:20,845 --> 00:18:22,263
So why are we talking about this?

319
00:18:22,388 --> 00:18:24,640
Well, you clearly missed
the departmental memo.

320
00:18:25,099 --> 00:18:26,976
They changed the policy
on office relationships.

321
00:18:27,101 --> 00:18:28,394
Don't have to lay low anymore.

322
00:18:28,853 --> 00:18:30,479
Hmm. Good for you.

323
00:18:31,105 --> 00:18:33,482
We could even get married
if we wanted to.

324
00:18:33,983 --> 00:18:35,026
Ah.

325
00:18:35,484 --> 00:18:36,652
That's it? "Ah"?

326
00:18:37,236 --> 00:18:38,571
She wants to get married
and you don't want to.

327
00:18:38,696 --> 00:18:40,406
No. Maybe.

328
00:18:40,823 --> 00:18:43,534
No. I don't know.
What do you think I should do?

329
00:18:43,659 --> 00:18:44,994
You wanna make it my fault?

330
00:18:46,495 --> 00:18:48,831
Okay, you're right.
It's my call.

331
00:18:59,967 --> 00:19:01,636
You're on private property.

332
00:19:03,095 --> 00:19:06,432
- CBI agents. You James Harris?
- That's right.

333
00:19:06,557 --> 00:19:09,268
You sell a rifle to a man named
Larry Kincaid about two weeks ago?

334
00:19:10,311 --> 00:19:12,897
It's quite possible.
I'd have to check my receipts.

335
00:19:13,022 --> 00:19:16,442
It's a violation of state law for you
to sell firearms without a license.

336
00:19:16,567 --> 00:19:19,612
- What are you hiding in the barn?
- My private arsenal.

337
00:19:21,447 --> 00:19:23,908
Open the door, please. Keep your hands
where we can see them.

338
00:19:29,622 --> 00:19:30,915
If I recall

339
00:19:31,791 --> 00:19:33,626
Larry bought one of these.

340
00:19:35,419 --> 00:19:38,673
A genuine 1840 Springfield musket.

341
00:19:38,798 --> 00:19:40,633
It's rigged up to fire blanks now.

342
00:19:44,261 --> 00:19:45,930
You're a civil war re-enactor.

343
00:19:46,222 --> 00:19:49,058
That's right, field commander
for the Napa Valley Cumberland guard

344
00:19:49,183 --> 00:19:50,518
156th regiment.

345
00:19:51,644 --> 00:19:53,771
We got a battle next week in fort point.

346
00:19:54,271 --> 00:19:56,732
That must be what the "156" meant
on the victim's shirt.

347
00:19:57,775 --> 00:19:58,943
What victim?

348
00:19:59,694 --> 00:20:02,655
Larry was murdered last night
behind the Bella Vista Chapel.

349
00:20:03,698 --> 00:20:05,282
Very sorry to hear that.

350
00:20:05,783 --> 00:20:08,452
You know of anyone who may have
wanted to hurt the victim?

351
00:20:08,911 --> 00:20:11,997
Oh, God, no. No.
Larry was a harmless sort.

352
00:20:12,623 --> 00:20:15,376
Quiet, but... a good soldier.

353
00:20:16,210 --> 00:20:19,296
His loss is gonna leave a real whole
in our little community.

354
00:20:23,175 --> 00:20:24,677
You break it, you buy it.

355
00:20:44,238 --> 00:20:46,782
Oh, please, don't stop praying
on my account.

356
00:20:47,032 --> 00:20:48,367
Oh, I'm not praying.

357
00:20:49,702 --> 00:20:50,786
Texting.

358
00:20:51,287 --> 00:20:53,789
In church.
Isn't that a sin?

359
00:20:54,373 --> 00:20:56,751
I hope not. I make my living
with my thumbs.

360
00:20:59,837 --> 00:21:02,798
I'm, uh, sorry for busting your wife.

361
00:21:04,341 --> 00:21:07,428
It's not the first drunken kiss
she's shared with a man that wasn't me.

362
00:21:07,553 --> 00:21:09,096
And I'm sure it won't be the last.

363
00:21:10,389 --> 00:21:12,600
Do you have any idea
who killed her brother?

364
00:21:13,267 --> 00:21:15,561
I believe it was someone
from the wedding party.

365
00:21:15,686 --> 00:21:17,563
No. No, you're wrong.

366
00:21:17,688 --> 00:21:19,190
No one had any reason
to kill uncle Larry.

367
00:21:19,315 --> 00:21:23,194
It would be like... killing a llama.
Why would you?

368
00:21:23,319 --> 00:21:24,612
They spit?

369
00:21:25,863 --> 00:21:27,156
What do you do for a living?

370
00:21:27,656 --> 00:21:29,033
I'm a speculator.

371
00:21:29,492 --> 00:21:32,828
- I look for undervalued assets.
- What kind of assets?

372
00:21:33,370 --> 00:21:36,791
Oh, artworks, islands...
anything I can buy low and sell high.

373
00:21:37,666 --> 00:21:41,962
Last month I found a first-edition
Coverdale Bible at a church auction

374
00:21:42,087 --> 00:21:43,672
printed in 1535.

375
00:21:44,131 --> 00:21:46,383
I was gonna do a reading from it
at the wedding.

376
00:21:49,553 --> 00:21:50,888
How much is that worth?

377
00:21:51,222 --> 00:21:54,558
- Oh, uh, half a million.
- Wow.

378
00:21:55,351 --> 00:21:57,019
For an antique bible.

379
00:21:59,939 --> 00:22:03,484
- Where is it now?
- With a private security firm.

380
00:22:03,609 --> 00:22:06,403
They were gonna deliver it here
for the wedding tonight.

381
00:22:07,363 --> 00:22:08,906
Might as well just sell it now.

382
00:22:15,996 --> 00:22:18,332
- Agent Van Pelt.
- Grace, I need your help.

383
00:22:18,457 --> 00:22:22,670
It is imperative that our young lovers
go ahead with the wedding as planned.

384
00:22:22,920 --> 00:22:24,839
Good luck with that.
You ruined a girl's wedding.

385
00:22:24,964 --> 00:22:28,467
Yes, and I lack the putty
to spackle those emotional wounds.

386
00:22:28,592 --> 00:22:29,927
That's why I'm calling you.

387
00:22:30,344 --> 00:22:33,514
- I have putty?
- No, you have life experience.

388
00:22:34,056 --> 00:22:38,185
Use it to convince the bride that her
"happily ever after" begins tonight.

389
00:22:39,436 --> 00:22:40,479
Really?

390
00:22:44,316 --> 00:22:47,528
Jane is feeling really badly
for what happened in the bull pen.

391
00:22:48,654 --> 00:22:49,864
He shouldn't.

392
00:22:50,155 --> 00:22:52,449
This was never going to work.
He did me a huge favor.

393
00:22:53,409 --> 00:22:55,286
Your families
don't want the marriage.

394
00:22:57,663 --> 00:22:59,665
Mom says I'm too smart
for Roberto.

395
00:23:00,124 --> 00:23:03,711
And my dad is worried he won't be able
to take care of me.

396
00:23:04,545 --> 00:23:07,548
And Roberto's parents just want him
to marry a nice catholic girl.

397
00:23:08,048 --> 00:23:09,258
What do you want?

398
00:23:13,554 --> 00:23:14,972
I want Roberto.

399
00:23:16,432 --> 00:23:17,474
But...

400
00:23:19,101 --> 00:23:20,561
It's too complicated.

401
00:23:21,770 --> 00:23:23,230
So you're gonna throw it away?

402
00:23:27,693 --> 00:23:29,320
Look...

403
00:23:32,114 --> 00:23:34,742
I had someone I wanted to spend
the rest of my life with.

404
00:23:36,452 --> 00:23:37,536
"But..."

405
00:23:39,079 --> 00:23:40,998
"Staying together would've ended
my career."

406
00:23:41,624 --> 00:23:42,958
"So I let him go."

407
00:23:44,752 --> 00:23:46,003
What happened?

408
00:23:47,171 --> 00:23:48,213
Oh...

409
00:23:49,798 --> 00:23:51,258
I got engaged to someone else.

410
00:23:53,385 --> 00:23:56,639
That ended badly.
So I shut down a little inside.

411
00:23:57,598 --> 00:24:01,185
"Closed some doors to feel safer.
But..."

412
00:24:02,019 --> 00:24:06,106
the problem is with every passing year
you close more doors.

413
00:24:07,066 --> 00:24:08,651
Until eventually...

414
00:24:09,902 --> 00:24:12,363
you wonder if anyone will ever
get inside again.

415
00:24:14,949 --> 00:24:17,701
- That's really tragic.
- Yeah. Well...

416
00:24:18,160 --> 00:24:19,495
that's why I keep the dress.

417
00:24:20,454 --> 00:24:23,749
- Your wedding dress?
- I know. It's crazy, right?

418
00:24:25,542 --> 00:24:26,585
But...

419
00:24:27,378 --> 00:24:29,338
It reminds me of who I was...

420
00:24:31,048 --> 00:24:33,968
Naive. Maybe a little foolish, but...

421
00:24:35,636 --> 00:24:36,804
"full of hope."

422
00:24:40,140 --> 00:24:42,476
I'm afraid if I destroy that dress...

423
00:24:45,604 --> 00:24:48,732
I'll forget what hope feels like.

424
00:24:59,284 --> 00:25:01,161
What are we gonna do
about McAllister?

425
00:25:01,662 --> 00:25:05,124
Well, until I get some alone time with him
I'm not gonna draw any conclusions.

426
00:25:05,290 --> 00:25:07,292
Well, he's avoided heights twice.

427
00:25:09,003 --> 00:25:12,548
If Red John is acrophobic,
why would he tell my shrink?

428
00:25:12,673 --> 00:25:14,967
To gain her trust
so he could steal her file...

429
00:25:15,092 --> 00:25:17,261
which he did right before he killed her.

430
00:25:17,886 --> 00:25:21,390
Guess what?
We're getting married. No hard feelings.

431
00:25:21,598 --> 00:25:24,393
Well, I think that's a great decision.
Congratulations.

432
00:25:24,518 --> 00:25:27,604
You... of course, you know
we will have to attend the ceremony

433
00:25:27,730 --> 00:25:31,567
just in case uncle Larry's killer
reveals himself.

434
00:25:31,692 --> 00:25:33,777
- Okay.
- Uh, you should follow us.

435
00:25:33,902 --> 00:25:36,196
- The courthouse closes.
- Courthouse?

436
00:25:36,321 --> 00:25:37,990
What about your big Napa wedding?

437
00:25:38,532 --> 00:25:40,367
It's a waste, but, I mean, what can we do?

438
00:25:40,492 --> 00:25:42,411
I can't find a priest.
I canceled him this morning.

439
00:25:42,536 --> 00:25:44,788
And we only have the chapel
for two more hours. So...

440
00:25:45,122 --> 00:25:49,668
Well, you know, it turns out that
I am, in fact a certified wedding officiant.

441
00:25:50,294 --> 00:25:51,795
- I can marry you.
- Oh, my gosh.

442
00:25:51,920 --> 00:25:54,089
- You're a lifesaver. Thank you.
- Thank you so much.

443
00:25:54,339 --> 00:25:55,716
It would be my pleasure.

444
00:25:57,217 --> 00:26:00,554
- Oh, you are not legally ordained.
- Yeah, I've never used it.

445
00:26:00,679 --> 00:26:02,181
But I always thought
it was a handy thing to have.

446
00:26:02,306 --> 00:26:04,308
- I don't believe you.
- I swear.

447
00:26:04,433 --> 00:26:06,018
I could marry you off in a minute.

448
00:26:07,061 --> 00:26:08,145
Hey, Ron.

449
00:26:08,395 --> 00:26:09,730
Don't tempt me.

450
00:26:17,235 --> 00:26:19,905
- "Sheriff McAllister."
- Yeah, it's Patrick Jane.

451
00:26:20,363 --> 00:26:22,115
"What can I do for you, Patrick?"

452
00:26:22,240 --> 00:26:24,326
Well, I'm at the chapel.
We have a break in the case.

453
00:26:24,451 --> 00:26:26,036
How soon can you meet me here?

454
00:26:26,161 --> 00:26:27,370
"Real soon."

455
00:26:28,747 --> 00:26:34,503
Funny thing, I, uh, saw your car
in the lot and I was just coming to find you.

456
00:26:36,588 --> 00:26:39,257
I didn't hear you coming.
You move quietly.

457
00:26:39,674 --> 00:26:42,594
Well, it's an old habit.
I've been a hunter most of my life.

458
00:26:42,844 --> 00:26:44,971
Really? What do you hunt?

459
00:26:45,639 --> 00:26:46,932
Anything with a face.

460
00:26:47,599 --> 00:26:48,725
Game is game, right?

461
00:26:49,726 --> 00:26:50,769
You hunt?

462
00:26:51,353 --> 00:26:53,688
Me? No.
No. Too squeamish. I...

463
00:26:53,814 --> 00:26:56,233
I couldn't handle all the skinning
and the gutting.

464
00:26:58,610 --> 00:27:01,154
Sure, yeah.
Clean and quarter a kill.

465
00:27:01,446 --> 00:27:05,033
- It takes some stomach for that.
- And a certain bloodlust.

466
00:27:07,119 --> 00:27:08,870
There's a grain of truth in that.

467
00:27:10,038 --> 00:27:13,375
So you got a break
in the duck pond killer case?

468
00:27:13,500 --> 00:27:17,546
Well... less a break, but, uh, more a plan.

469
00:27:17,963 --> 00:27:23,468
I'm gonna reveal the killer tonight. And
I need you and your people to just hang back.

470
00:27:23,593 --> 00:27:24,719
No problem.

471
00:27:25,220 --> 00:27:27,597
I truly appreciate you making
my job easier for me.

472
00:27:37,023 --> 00:27:38,150
You look great.

473
00:27:38,817 --> 00:27:39,943
Thank you, Wayne.

474
00:27:42,737 --> 00:27:45,365
Jane thinks our man's gonna try
to sneak out of the reception.

475
00:27:45,490 --> 00:27:47,159
We need to be there.
I'm gonna follow him.

476
00:27:47,284 --> 00:27:49,703
What if he's too cautious to make
a move while we're here?

477
00:27:49,828 --> 00:27:52,164
If he's in the reception tent
for more than two hours

478
00:27:52,289 --> 00:27:54,583
Jane wants you to create
some sort of distraction.

479
00:27:54,708 --> 00:27:56,084
He thinks it'll encourage him out.

480
00:27:56,209 --> 00:27:57,919
- What kind of distraction?
- You know what?

481
00:27:58,044 --> 00:28:01,423
- Don't worry. I can handle this.
- Great. Great. Let's split up.

482
00:28:11,850 --> 00:28:14,394
"For this thing shall a man leave
his father and mother"

483
00:28:14,728 --> 00:28:16,271
"and cleave unto his wife"

484
00:28:16,855 --> 00:28:19,274
"and the twain shall become
one flesh."

485
00:28:19,399 --> 00:28:20,984
Whoa. That sounds messy...

486
00:28:21,693 --> 00:28:22,736
but beautiful.

487
00:28:23,987 --> 00:28:25,530
Thank you, Mr. Bonner.

488
00:28:25,655 --> 00:28:28,533
Reading from the very rare
and valuable Coverdale Bible.

489
00:28:28,992 --> 00:28:33,580
Scripture... tick.
Rings... we've, uh, we've done that.

490
00:28:34,664 --> 00:28:36,875
- Have you ever done this before?
- Have you?

491
00:28:37,000 --> 00:28:40,378
- Yes. I'm married.
- What's next?

492
00:28:41,046 --> 00:28:43,882
- Kissing.
- That's what I thought.

493
00:28:44,633 --> 00:28:45,967
Okay, um...

494
00:28:47,427 --> 00:28:49,512
If there are no objections...

495
00:28:51,973 --> 00:28:53,350
- Nope.
- Okay.

496
00:28:54,559 --> 00:28:56,519
By the power vested in me...

497
00:28:57,896 --> 00:29:04,236
by a mail-order ministry, I...
I now pronounce you husband and wife.

498
00:29:04,569 --> 00:29:07,864
Roberto...
you may kiss the bride.

499
00:29:22,295 --> 00:29:23,630
Be careful.

500
00:29:24,422 --> 00:29:27,050
I'll, uh...
I'll guard that for you, if you'd like.

501
00:29:27,342 --> 00:29:30,136
You wish.
Mr. Bonner trusted me to lock this away

502
00:29:30,262 --> 00:29:33,848
in the priest's room for the reception, so...
step back, son.

503
00:29:48,697 --> 00:29:49,864
Excuse me?

504
00:29:50,407 --> 00:29:53,785
- Thank you. Try the cake.
- Oh, thank you.

505
00:29:54,244 --> 00:29:57,580
It's been two hours and nobody's going
anywhere near the exit.

506
00:29:57,706 --> 00:29:59,874
- We need a distraction.
- Okay.

507
00:30:00,417 --> 00:30:01,626
How do I look?

508
00:30:01,751 --> 00:30:04,004
- Fine. Why?
- Good.

509
00:30:07,382 --> 00:30:09,134
Excuse me? Is this on?

510
00:30:09,592 --> 00:30:10,635
Yeah.

511
00:30:11,761 --> 00:30:13,346
Uh, excuse me, ladies and gentlemen

512
00:30:13,471 --> 00:30:16,349
if I can I have your attention,
for, uh, one second, please?

513
00:30:16,474 --> 00:30:18,727
I'd like to offer one more toast.

514
00:30:20,103 --> 00:30:22,647
I'd like to thank Stacey and Roberto

515
00:30:22,772 --> 00:30:26,318
for making me realize
just how important it is to protect a dream.

516
00:30:30,405 --> 00:30:33,366
I didn't protect my dream.
To be honest, I, uh...

517
00:30:33,867 --> 00:30:34,993
well, I didn't know how.

518
00:30:35,118 --> 00:30:38,371
But what can you do when you meet
the right person at the wrong time?

519
00:30:38,496 --> 00:30:39,956
Do her anyway!

520
00:30:40,457 --> 00:30:43,460
Well, yeah, that works...
for a while. But...

521
00:30:44,210 --> 00:30:46,463
eventually you say things

522
00:30:47,672 --> 00:30:49,174
or don't say things.

523
00:30:49,924 --> 00:30:53,511
You, uh, make more mistakes
than you can count.

524
00:30:55,263 --> 00:30:56,556
And in the end

525
00:30:56,848 --> 00:30:57,891
well...

526
00:30:59,809 --> 00:31:01,186
things fall apart.

527
00:31:02,187 --> 00:31:05,315
- Is this your idea of a distraction?
- Trust me.

528
00:31:06,274 --> 00:31:07,817
Now if you're lucky

529
00:31:08,818 --> 00:31:10,362
you might find each other again.

530
00:31:11,404 --> 00:31:14,074
But there's too much history
and heartache

531
00:31:14,199 --> 00:31:16,076
to ever find your way back
to that dream.

532
00:31:17,952 --> 00:31:19,245
At least that's what I thought.

533
00:31:20,914 --> 00:31:23,375
I'm hoping... I was wrong.

534
00:31:27,587 --> 00:31:28,671
Grace...

535
00:31:31,299 --> 00:31:32,759
will you marry me?

536
00:31:35,595 --> 00:31:37,430
Well, say something. This is real.

537
00:31:38,473 --> 00:31:40,642
Yes, Wayne.
Of course I'll marry you.

538
00:31:57,492 --> 00:31:59,035
Target's on the move.

539
00:32:15,176 --> 00:32:16,845
Oh, that was easy.

540
00:32:20,515 --> 00:32:23,643
- How'd you know I'd be here?
- You know, the big fight at the CBI

541
00:32:23,768 --> 00:32:27,272
you were the only one that wanted
the wedding to go ahead as planned.

542
00:32:27,730 --> 00:32:30,567
Ergo, you were the guilty party.

543
00:32:31,443 --> 00:32:33,695
Guilty? I was just checking on the bible.
I didn't do anything.

544
00:32:33,820 --> 00:32:35,738
- You killed a man.
- What?

545
00:32:35,864 --> 00:32:38,366
Oh, Charlie, stop.
You're just embarrassing yourself.

546
00:32:38,491 --> 00:32:41,411
There's only one way you could Jimmy
a dead bolt with a putty knife.

547
00:32:41,911 --> 00:32:43,246
You sawed through the bolt.

548
00:32:43,371 --> 00:32:45,540
So you snuck out of the party but...

549
00:32:45,665 --> 00:32:47,542
somebody caught you
in the act, didn't they?

550
00:32:52,005 --> 00:32:53,047
Charlie?

551
00:32:53,673 --> 00:32:54,883
What are you doing here, Larry?

552
00:32:55,592 --> 00:32:57,469
I came to swipe the priest's wine.

553
00:32:57,594 --> 00:33:00,096
We ran out at the party.
Pretty smart, huh?

554
00:33:00,805 --> 00:33:01,931
Uh, what are you doing here?

555
00:33:03,266 --> 00:33:04,350
Is that a saw?

556
00:33:05,351 --> 00:33:06,895
You're gonna steal
the bible, aren't you?

557
00:33:07,353 --> 00:33:08,897
You know how much
that thing is worth?

558
00:33:09,481 --> 00:33:10,815
Larry, it's insured. All right?

559
00:33:10,940 --> 00:33:13,026
Mr. Bonner will get his money,
and we'll get rich.

560
00:33:13,485 --> 00:33:14,861
That's right, I'll cut you in.

561
00:33:14,986 --> 00:33:17,447
Okay, you'll never have to deliver
another pizza in your life.

562
00:33:18,531 --> 00:33:19,949
I kinda like delivering pizzas.

563
00:33:20,450 --> 00:33:22,994
- I'm going back to the party now.
- Larry, wait!

564
00:33:23,536 --> 00:33:25,121
Well, you're not gonna
tell them, are you?

565
00:33:25,246 --> 00:33:27,957
No, I...
I'm just going back. Bye.

566
00:33:34,339 --> 00:33:35,965
I don't suppose you wanted to kill him

567
00:33:36,090 --> 00:33:39,052
but, uh, if wishes were horses
and all that...

568
00:33:39,636 --> 00:33:41,763
And you not being from around here

569
00:33:41,888 --> 00:33:44,474
you didn't know about the cliff
so you threw the body in the pond.

570
00:33:44,933 --> 00:33:46,559
I suppose you took
the wallet and I.D.

571
00:33:46,684 --> 00:33:48,561
because you wanted to make it
look like a robbery.

572
00:33:49,062 --> 00:33:50,688
You can put down
the putty knife now.

573
00:33:53,691 --> 00:33:54,817
Whatever.

574
00:33:55,902 --> 00:33:57,654
I'll leave this up to my lawyers.
Just...

575
00:33:58,029 --> 00:33:59,781
- don't make the handcuffs too tight, okay?
- Oh, no, no.

576
00:33:59,906 --> 00:34:01,699
- I don't do the cuffing...
- Jane, move away!

577
00:34:02,992 --> 00:34:06,204
- Charlie, put the gun down.
- My dad died in jail.

578
00:34:06,329 --> 00:34:07,497
I'll take my chances
on the outside, thank you.

579
00:34:07,622 --> 00:34:08,665
You know, if you just let me go

580
00:34:08,790 --> 00:34:11,251
I'm sure they'd be happy
to open the dialogue with you.

581
00:34:11,376 --> 00:34:14,170
Charlie, stay calm.
You don't wanna go down this road.

582
00:34:14,337 --> 00:34:15,547
No, you don't.

583
00:34:18,049 --> 00:34:19,259
Get away from me!

584
00:34:21,678 --> 00:34:23,179
You wanna live?
Get me outta here.

585
00:34:23,304 --> 00:34:24,764
Upstairs, now! Go!

586
00:34:32,105 --> 00:34:33,982
Charlie, think about what you're doing!

587
00:34:36,109 --> 00:34:37,902
- What's through the door?
- No idea.

588
00:34:38,027 --> 00:34:40,613
I'll cover it. Set up an exterior perimeter
with Rigsby and Van Pelt

589
00:34:40,738 --> 00:34:42,073
and call for backup.

590
00:35:03,434 --> 00:35:04,644
Keep moving.

591
00:35:04,769 --> 00:35:07,772
Uh, yeah, I'd... I'd love to, Charlie
but without wings...

592
00:35:07,897 --> 00:35:09,607
On the roof. Now!

593
00:35:11,192 --> 00:35:13,486
- We're getting outta here.
- Okay.

594
00:35:21,952 --> 00:35:23,245
Whoa!

595
00:35:31,379 --> 00:35:33,589
You know if there's
a-a way down from here?

596
00:35:33,714 --> 00:35:36,717
- We're gonna find a way down.
- Yeah, that's what I was afraid of.

597
00:35:38,010 --> 00:35:39,053
Uh...

598
00:35:41,013 --> 00:35:42,264
Hey!

599
00:35:45,059 --> 00:35:46,185
Drop the gun.

600
00:35:53,609 --> 00:35:55,778
Now he's talking directly to Jesus.

601
00:35:57,530 --> 00:36:00,116
Where... where did you come from?

602
00:36:06,497 --> 00:36:09,041
The church keeps an extension ladder
for tree trimming.

603
00:36:14,672 --> 00:36:17,758
Don't look down.
Bad place for the spins.

604
00:36:23,347 --> 00:36:24,557
Give me your hand.

605
00:36:33,649 --> 00:36:34,734
Let's go.

606
00:36:41,282 --> 00:36:44,076
Good thing I ignored your request
to stay away, huh?

607
00:36:44,201 --> 00:36:46,078
Guess I just felt obliged
to keep an eye on you.

608
00:36:46,871 --> 00:36:50,541
- Yeah. Why?
- Truth is, I didn't trust ya.

609
00:36:50,666 --> 00:36:52,209
The way you forced your way
onto the case here...

610
00:36:52,334 --> 00:36:54,253
it almost seemed like you had
a hidden agenda.

611
00:36:55,671 --> 00:36:58,674
Ah, well, sometimes
I give out mixed messages.

612
00:36:58,799 --> 00:37:00,509
It's one of my many failings.

613
00:37:00,760 --> 00:37:03,345
- It looks like everybody's okay?
- Sure.

614
00:37:03,471 --> 00:37:05,931
He saved my life
out there on the roof.

615
00:37:06,056 --> 00:37:07,433
My pleasure.

616
00:37:08,309 --> 00:37:10,311
I'm at your disposal anytime.

617
00:37:10,811 --> 00:37:12,313
I'll see you two downstairs.

618
00:37:12,772 --> 00:37:14,565
Hey, get it out! Get it out!

619
00:37:18,861 --> 00:37:21,822
I didn't see that coming.
I hate those things.

620
00:37:23,073 --> 00:37:25,576
- You're not hurt?
- Oh, I'm fine, Lisbon. Fine.

621
00:37:25,951 --> 00:37:27,745
Well, you missed all the action,
you know.

622
00:37:27,870 --> 00:37:30,748
Oh, I did? Did you not see me
out there on the roof?

623
00:37:30,998 --> 00:37:33,083
- That was some action.
- This is different.

624
00:37:33,501 --> 00:37:35,294
It looks like we're gonna be spending
a little bit more time

625
00:37:35,419 --> 00:37:37,171
in Napa than we expected.

626
00:37:38,005 --> 00:37:39,048
Really?

627
00:38:02,613 --> 00:38:04,406
Join hands and face each other, please.

628
00:38:06,283 --> 00:38:07,701
Grace, you'll begin.

629
00:38:08,202 --> 00:38:09,411
Repeat after me.

630
00:38:10,079 --> 00:38:11,705
- I, Grace...
- I, Grace...

631
00:38:11,831 --> 00:38:13,874
- take you, Wayne...
- take you, Wayne...

632
00:38:14,291 --> 00:38:15,543
to be my husband.

633
00:38:16,001 --> 00:38:18,379
To have and to hold
from this day forward...

634
00:38:20,589 --> 00:38:21,841
for better or for worse

635
00:38:22,466 --> 00:38:24,093
for richer or for poorer

636
00:38:24,760 --> 00:38:26,303
in sickness and in health

637
00:38:27,471 --> 00:38:28,931
to love and to cherish...

638
00:38:30,140 --> 00:38:31,559
until death do us part.

639
00:38:32,601 --> 00:38:34,019
You seem to have that down.

640
00:38:35,145 --> 00:38:36,897
This has been a long time coming.

641
00:38:37,022 --> 00:38:40,150
- And now you, Wayne.
- Uh, everything she just said.

642
00:38:41,235 --> 00:38:42,278
Grace...

643
00:38:42,862 --> 00:38:44,405
no more wasted time.

644
00:38:44,905 --> 00:38:46,907
I wanna spend every day with you.

645
00:38:47,449 --> 00:38:49,451
Every day for the rest of our lives

646
00:38:49,577 --> 00:38:52,079
and... whatever comes after.

647
00:38:52,454 --> 00:38:55,040
Love freely given has no beginning
or end.

648
00:38:55,499 --> 00:38:58,085
Your rings represent
your unending love for each other.

649
00:38:58,544 --> 00:39:00,212
May I have the ring for Grace, please?

650
00:39:06,635 --> 00:39:07,678
Thanks.

651
00:39:08,262 --> 00:39:10,890
Place that ring on Grace's finger
and repeat after me.

652
00:39:12,266 --> 00:39:14,768
I give you this ring as a symbol of my love.

653
00:39:14,894 --> 00:39:18,230
Grace, I give you this ring
as a symbol of my love.

654
00:39:18,355 --> 00:39:20,274
With this ring, I marry you.

655
00:39:21,358 --> 00:39:24,737
With this ring... I marry you.

656
00:39:25,863 --> 00:39:27,656
May we have the ring
for Wayne, please?

657
00:39:30,743 --> 00:39:32,745
- I think you know what to do.
- Yeah.

658
00:39:33,621 --> 00:39:36,540
Wayne, I give you this ring
as a symbol of my love.

659
00:39:37,625 --> 00:39:39,543
With this ring, I marry you.

660
00:39:42,922 --> 00:39:44,757
In as much as the two of you
have made known

661
00:39:44,882 --> 00:39:47,885
your mutual desire
and your consent to be married

662
00:39:48,010 --> 00:39:50,220
by virtue of the authority invested in me

663
00:39:50,763 --> 00:39:54,558
I hereby pronounce you
husband and wife.

664
00:39:54,892 --> 00:39:56,143
You may kiss the bride.

665
00:40:07,947 --> 00:40:10,240
I'm sorry it's not the fairy tale
you dreamed of.

666
00:40:11,742 --> 00:40:13,535
I'll take the real thing any day.

667
00:40:20,542 --> 00:40:21,752
Hey, guys...

668
00:40:23,796 --> 00:40:25,214
your ride to the hotel is ready.

669
00:40:25,881 --> 00:40:27,257
We have a ride?

670
00:40:29,051 --> 00:40:30,594
We have a ride.

671
00:40:38,394 --> 00:40:40,646
Twenty-one-gun salute formation.

672
00:40:43,190 --> 00:40:44,316
Ready!

673
00:40:46,527 --> 00:40:48,070
Aim!

674
00:40:48,612 --> 00:40:50,072
Fire!

675
00:40:50,406 --> 00:40:52,616
- I think that's our cue.
- Ready!

676
00:40:53,784 --> 00:40:54,952
Aim!

677
00:40:55,452 --> 00:40:56,662
Fire!

678
00:40:57,538 --> 00:40:58,664
Ready!

679
00:40:59,248 --> 00:41:00,290
Aim!

680
00:41:00,791 --> 00:41:02,001
Fire!

